# harness: rpc --shipped / (pinned) / --stack-default, and --nagle at each
# date:                  2026-09-20
# runtime:               .NET 8.0.31, 4 processors
# transport:             unix domain socket, P2.2 (540,422 B) downstream
# rounds:                5, best-of, and the whole sweep run TWICE
# code:                  poc/csharp/src/Rpc/Program.cs
#
# R14 pointed at the transport. The RPC arm has carried two transport rows since
# stage 15 -- ArmoniK's pinned window and .NET's stack default -- and this stage
# establishes, from the shipped packages rather than from a description, that
# NEITHER OF THEM IS WHAT PRODUCTION RUNS.


================================================================================
1. WHAT THE SHIPPED CLIENTS ACTUALLY CONFIGURE, READ FROM THE PACKAGES
================================================================================

**`packages/rust/armonik-transport`, `src/config.rs:10`.** `ClientConfig` carries
`endpoint`, `allow_unsafe_connection`, `identity`, `cacert`, `override_target`,
`connect_timeout`, `timeout`, `rate_limit`, `tcp_keepalive`,
`tcp_keepalive_interval`, `tcp_keepalive_retries`, `tcp_nagle_algorithm`,
`http2_keep_alive_interval`, `http2_keep_alive_timeout`,
`http2_keep_alive_while_idle`, `http2_max_header_list_size` and `user_agent`.

**There is no stream-window and no connection-window field**, and `grep -rn
window src/` over the package returns nothing at all. `tcp_nagle_algorithm` is
documented "Enable Nagle's algorithm (disable TCP_NODELAY), defaults to false",
read from `GrpcClient__TcpNagleAlgorithm` (`config.rs:152`) and applied as
`http.set_nodelay(!config.tcp_nagle_algorithm)` (`connect.rs:127`). So the Rust
client ships with Nagle OFF and no window.

**`packages/csharp`, `ArmoniK.Api.Common.Channel/Utils/GrpcChannelProvider.cs:88`
-- and this is the part a description would not have got right.** The C# client
sets no window either, but on the UNIX SOCKET path it DOES set

    AppContext.SetSwitch("System.Net.SocketsHttpHandler.Http2FlowControl."
                         + "DisableDynamicWindowSizing", true);

under a comment naming it a **workaround for a connectivity issue**
(grpc-dotnet#2361). It is not a throughput decision.

**So production on .NET is a THIRD configuration, and this arm had never
measured it**: no window pinned, .NET's 64 KB default, and the auto-tuner that
would otherwise grow that window to 16 MB switched off. The window is left at
65,535 bytes for a 540,422-byte message, with nothing to grow it.

  * `--pinned` (this arm's old headline) is ArmoniK's INTENDED configuration and
    **no ArmoniK client ships it**.
  * `--stack-default` is .NET's, and ArmoniK does not ship that either, because
    it turns the auto-tuner off.
  * `--shipped` is the R14 baseline and is new here.


================================================================================
2. THE SHIPPED CONFIGURATION IS THE WORST OF THE THREE
================================================================================

`gp-marshaller` (R14's codec baseline), CPU us per call, the whole sweep run
twice:

    in flight    SHIPPED        INTENDED (4 MiB)   .NET stack default
    1            7,600 / 7,000  5,550 / 5,350      5,450 / 5,600
    8            6,050 / 6,050  5,250 / 5,350      5,900 / 5,750
    16           7,604 / 7,448  6,250 / 7,656      7,292 / 7,656

**At 1 in flight the shipped configuration costs 27 to 39 percent more CPU per
call than either alternative, in both runs. At 8 it costs 13 to 15 percent more
than the intended one. At 16 the three are indistinguishable** -- the intended
column alone moved 6,250 to 7,656 between runs, which is larger than any gap at
that level.

**The mechanism is confirmed and it is the window.** The same three
configurations with NO CODEC AT ALL (the `byte[]` passthrough both ways), at two
payload sizes, wall us per call:

    payload        SHIPPED    INTENDED   stack default
    858 B            149.2       142.9           143.0
    540,422 B      1,689.6     1,197.5         1,376.1

**Identical on the small payload and 23 to 41 percent apart on the large one.**
A cost that appears only when the message exceeds the window is flow control and
cannot be anything else; a payload-independent cause would have moved both rows.

**And the shape of it is the point.** .NET's auto-tuner, left alone, gets most of
the way to the pinned window (1,376 against 1,197). The shipped configuration
turns the auto-tuner OFF and puts nothing in its place, so it is worse than
either doing nothing or pinning: **1,689 against 1,376 for the stack default it
overrides.** The workaround costs what it costs because it is half a change.


================================================================================
3. WHAT THIS MEANS FOR THIS SLICE'S OWN NUMBERS, AND FOR THE REPORT
================================================================================

**Every ratio this slice has published is unaffected**, because a ratio is taken
between arms that ran under the SAME transport configuration, and the codec is
the only thing that varied. The transport is common-mode and divides out.

**The absolutes move, and the headline row should be relabelled.** Stage 15 and
stage 18's "pinned" rows are ArmoniK's intended configuration, not production's.
Under R14 the baseline is what production runs, so the shipped row is the
headline and the pinned one is the labelled deliberate divergence -- which is the
opposite of how stage 15 labelled them.

**The actionable finding is one line of C#**, and it is the only thing in this
slice that is a change to `packages/` rather than to the exploration:
`GrpcChannelProvider` already sets the switch that disables the auto-tuner, and
setting `InitialHttp2StreamWindowSize` beside it would recover 27 to 39 percent
of CPU per call at low concurrency on the payload this branch measures. **Not
proposing the edit** -- `ffi/CLAUDE.md` says nothing under `packages/` changes --
but the measurement belongs in the report, and it is larger than every codec
difference this slice has found in a real RPC.

**And the window discussion is a recommendation, not a baseline.** No ArmoniK
client pins an HTTP/2 window: the Rust package has no field for it and the C#
package does not set one. The 4 MiB figure is an intention.


================================================================================
WHAT IS NOT MEASURED
================================================================================

  * The connectivity issue the workaround exists for (grpc-dotnet#2361). Whether
    pinning a window beside the switch reintroduces it is not tested here and is
    the only reason the one-line change is a recommendation rather than a fix.
  * The server side. Kestrel runs at its own defaults in the shipped and
    stack-default arms, as it does in production.
  * Loopback TCP under the shipped configuration. `GrpcChannelProvider` sets the
    switch only on the UDS path, so a TCP client ships with the auto-tuner ON --
    i.e. production's TCP row is `--stack-default`, and that IS measured.
  * The window's effect at 16 in flight, which is inside the spread here.
  * Absolutes. R13.


